MESG(1)                        P32W Unix                         MESG(1)

NAME
       mesg - allow or refuse messages

SYNOPSIS
       mesg [y | n]

DESCRIPTION
       mesg y lets others write to your terminal with write(1) and
       wall(1); mesg n stops them. With no argument mesg says which
       it is: is y or is n.

       The terminal is the first of the standard input, output and
       error that is open on one of /dev/tty0 ... /dev/tty9.
       Messages are allowed while its group-write permission
       (020) is on: login(1) gives you the terminal, owned by you,
       group tty, mode 0620, so messages start allowed. write and
       wall are set-group-id tty, which lets them write to a
       terminal with that bit on. mesg changes only that bit
       (with chmod(2)), which as the terminal's owner you may do.

EXAMPLES
       $ mesg
       is y
       $ mesg n                        no interruptions, please

EXIT STATUS
       0 if messages are allowed (after the change, if any); 1 if
       they are refused; 2 for an error: a usage error, no
       terminal, or a terminal that is not yours.

SEE ALSO
       write(1), wall(1), login(1), chmod(1), stty(1)

BUGS
       Root reaches every terminal with wall regardless. After su,
       the terminal still belongs to the user who logged in, so
       mesg y or n fails for the new user (except root). A
       terminal opened as /dev/tty is not recognised.
